<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Qara Khitai</h1><p class="st-meta">1124 CE – 1218 CE · capital Balasagun</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Khitan empire founded in Central Asia by refugees from the fallen Liao dynasty, whose defeat of the Seljuks inspired European legends of Prester John.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> destroyed the Khitan <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1125, the prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-dashi">Yelü Dashi</a> fled west with followers. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasagun">Balasagun</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-kara-khanid-khanate">Eastern Kara-Khanid Khanate</a> around 1134, proclaimed himself gurkhan and founded the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>, known as Qara Khitai.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The gurkhans ruled a mostly Muslim population through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> dynasties such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanids</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-qocho">Kingdom of Qocho</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmshahs</a>, collecting tribute instead of governing directly. The court kept Chinese-style titles and reign names while tolerating all religions. In 1141 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-dashi">Yelü Dashi</a> crushed the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar">Ahmad Sanjar</a> at the Qatwan steppe near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, news of which reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prester-john">Prester John</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1211 the Naiman prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuchlug">Kuchlug</a>, a refugee from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, seized the throne, persecuting Muslims. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> took its western lands, and in 1218 Mongol forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> killed Kuchlug and absorbed Qara Khitai.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/qara-khitai.jpg" alt="Qara Khitai" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Liao.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1124 CE – 1218 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Balasagun</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yelü Dashi, Yelü Zhilugu, Kuchlug</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Sunni Islam, Church of the East</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khitan, Chinese, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a href="/countries/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, <a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Western Liao, Kara Khitan Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1171&amp;focus=qara-khitai" aria-label="Qara Khitai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6500 659 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Qara Khitai lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6820 915L7137 873M6820 915L6652 1014M6820 915L6877 1020M6820 915L7866 1006M6820 915L7559 780"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7137 873h0M6652 1014h0M6877 1020h0M7866 1006h0M7559 780h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6820 915h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6820 915h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Balasagun. <a href="/atlas?year=1171&amp;focus=qara-khitai">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
